based on all the comments above, this is the hacky fix I have, which seems to work. 

If you have a better solution, please share :)

```rust
if env::var("APPIMAGE").is_ok() || std::env::var("FLATPAK_ID").is_ok() {
    return;
}

let desktop = env::var("XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP").unwrap_or_default().to_lowercase();
if desktop.contains("gnome") {
    env::set_var("GDK_BACKEND", "x11");
}
```

@participant5 i tested the new release and that's not really a solution. The custom header has 2 serious problems:

1. the header does not follow the platform theme (light/dark);
2. the size of the window is wrong because the header height is not taken into account. This means that the available area is different between platforms with Linux Wayland getting less vertical space than, for example, Windows.

Please, reopen this bug report until a proper fix is implemented.

I found the root cause of this issue when troubleshooting the issue https://github.com/tauri-apps/tao/issues/929. Adding a comment here for future reference.

It turns out to be an issue of the GTK or Wayland under certain scenarios.

By tracking the history, I found that this issue was introduced by #941, which is trying to fix the duplicated mouse event triggered by a single click on Linux. The solution in this PR is to prevent the related event from being propagated.

Since the issue is Wayland-specific. I reproduced this issue with a minimal GTK app using the `gtk-rs` crate.

```rust
use gtk::{glib, prelude::*};
use gtk::{Application, ApplicationWindow};

fn main() {
  let app = Application::builder()
    .application_id("org.example.HelloWorld")
    .build();

  app.connect_activate(|app| {
    // We create the main window.
    let win = ApplicationWindow::builder()
      .application(app)
      .default_width(320)
      .default_height(320)
      .title("Hello, World!")
      .build();

    win.connect_button_press_event(|_win, event| {
      dbg!(event);
      glib::Propagation::Stop
    });

    win.show_all();
  });

  app.run();
}
```

On Wayland, running the above code directly will cause the window to not move. If you run the code with `GDK_BACKEND=x11` (i.e., XWayland is used) or on X11, the window can move.

The reason is that clicking on the window title bar will trigger a button press event on Wayland, and the `connect_button_press_event` callback stops the event. So the window cannot move. However, on XWayland or X11, clicking on the window title bar will not trigger a button press event, so the window can move.

So, this could be a bug in GTK or Wayland. I have reported it at: https://<redacted-host>/GNOME/gtk/-/issues/7327

----

BTW, using `GDK_BACKEND=x11` will use XWayland on the Wayland environment. If you enable Tauri's tray icon, this will cause the two system tray icons (one is active, another is dead) problem.
